course: euphemisms
lesson: 24
title: Soft words for hard things: politics and government spin
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Governments must explain hard choices, such as cuts, wars and camps, to the people, and soft words make those choices easier to swallow.
explain: Sometimes the soft word is fair and polite; often it is spin that hides who did what, and to whom.
explain: "Mistakes were made" is the classic example: it admits something went wrong but never says who made the mistakes.
explain: Some words hide terrible things: governments have called prison camps "vocational training centres".
explain: Ask of every official phrase: who is doing what, to whom, and what would the plain word be?
fact: "Mistakes were made" means we did wrong, but we will not say who.
fact: "Plausible deniability" means an arrangement that lets leaders deny they knew about something.
fact: "Regime change" means overthrowing (forcing out) a foreign government.
fact: "Nation-building" means occupying a country (moving in with soldiers and taking control) and remaking its government.
fact: "Public diplomacy" can mean government propaganda aimed at foreigners: one-sided messages made to get people to think well of a country.
fact: "Fiscal consolidation" means cuts in public spending and tax rises.
fact: "Austerity measures" means cuts in public spending.
fact: "Efficiency savings" means budget cuts (UK).
fact: "Entitlement reform" means cutting pensions and welfare (US).
fact: "Tax relief" means a tax cut.
fact: "Investment" in a politician's mouth often just means government spending.
fact: "Irregular migrant" means a migrant (someone who moves to live in another country) without legal permission to enter or stay.
fact: "Undocumented immigrant" means an immigrant living without legal permission (US, polite).
fact: "Internally displaced person" means someone forced from home who is still inside their own country.
fact: "Vocational training centre" (vocational means about learning a job) has been used as a name for an internment camp, a camp where people are locked up without a fair trial.
fact: "Re-education" means forced political indoctrination: people are made to repeat and accept the government's ideas, often in camps.
fact: "Population transfer" means forcing people out of their homes and moving them elsewhere.
fact: "A full and frank exchange of views" means a serious argument.
fact: "Constructive talks" often means talks that reached no agreement.
fact: "We are reviewing our options" often means we have not decided anything.
exercise: What does "mistakes were made" hide? | Who made the mistakes | That anything went wrong | How long ago it was | Which day it happened
exercise: What is "plausible deniability"? | An arrangement that lets leaders deny they knew | A believable excuse for being late to an important meeting | A law that forbids lying | A promise to tell the truth
exercise: What does "regime change" mean in foreign policy? | Overthrowing a foreign government | Changing a diet plan | Holding a normal election in a foreign land | Changing the school timetable
exercise: What did "nation-building" mean when a powerful country used it abroad? | Occupying a country and remaking its government | Building new houses and roads at home for everyone | Teaching history in schools | Drawing a new map for fun
exercise: What can "public diplomacy" really mean? | Government propaganda aimed at foreigners | Talks held outdoors in a big public park | Diplomats who are very polite | Open votes in parliament
exercise: What does "fiscal consolidation" mean? | Spending cuts and tax rises | Joining two big banks together into one | Printing more money | Building a new treasury
exercise: What are "austerity measures"? | Cuts in public spending | Strict rules for school uniforms | Measuring tapes for builders | Extra spending on hospitals
exercise: What are "efficiency savings" in a UK council budget? | Budget cuts | Money saved by turning off lights | A bonus for fast workers | A new savings account
exercise: What does "entitlement reform" mean in US politics? | Cutting pensions and welfare | Giving people new titles | Raising pensions for everyone | Changing the names of laws
exercise: What is "tax relief"? | A tax cut | Medicine for stress about taxes | A new tax | A tax on sweets
exercise: What does a politician often mean by "investment" in schools? | Government spending | Buying shares in a company | Putting money in a bank | Selling schools to firms
exercise: What is an "irregular migrant"? | A migrant without legal permission to enter or stay | A migrant who travels only on odd days of the month | A migrant who is rarely seen | A bird that flies off course
exercise: What does "undocumented immigrant" mean in the US? | An immigrant living without legal permission | An immigrant who lost a library card | An immigrant who writes no diaries | An immigrant with no phone
exercise: Who is an "internally displaced person"? | Someone forced from home but still in their own country | Someone who moved to a new room in the same house | Someone who is ill inside | Someone who works indoors
exercise: What has a "vocational training centre" been used to hide? | An internment camp | A school for cooking | A driving school | A job centre
exercise: What does "re-education" mean when a government sends people away for it? | Forced political indoctrination | Going back to school by choice | A second university degree | Extra lessons in maths
exercise: What does "population transfer" mean? | Forcing people out of their homes and moving them | Counting all the people of a town, house by house | Moving to a new house by choice | Changing buses on a journey
exercise: What does "a full and frank exchange of views" mean in a diplomatic report? | A serious argument | A friendly chat over dinner | Swapping holiday photos | A long agreement
exercise: What do "constructive talks" often mean? | Talks that reached no agreement | Talks about building roads | Talks that solved everything | Talks held on a building site
exercise: What does "we are reviewing our options" often mean? | We have not decided anything | We have chosen a plan | We are reading film reviews | We are closing down
test: News: "After a full and frank exchange of views, the two leaders left without a statement." What happened at the meeting? | They argued seriously | They agreed on everything | They swapped gifts | They went for a walk
test: A minister says: "Mistakes were made, and lessons will be learned." What has he avoided saying? | Who made the mistakes | That anything went wrong | That lessons exist | What day it is
test: "council's calling it efficiency savings, means the library's shut, innit" — what does the speaker understand? | Efficiency savings are budget cuts | The library got cheaper lights | The library is being enlarged | The council saved money by luck
test: A government announces "austerity measures". What will most people notice? | Less public spending on services | More free buses for everyone | New hospitals and schools being built | Higher pensions
test: Report: "Thousands of internally displaced persons sheltered in schools." Which is true? | They fled their homes but stayed in their country | They moved abroad to another country to find work | They were tourists | They were teachers
test: A spokesman says: "We are reviewing our options." What has the government decided? | Nothing yet | To go to war | To cut taxes | To resign
test: The leaders held "constructive talks". Did they reach agreement? | Probably not | Yes, on every point | Yes, they signed a treaty | No, they never met
test: "they called it re-education but nobody could leave" — what was it really? | Forced political indoctrination | A holiday camp in the mountains | A school trip | A night class
test: Sam says: "Tax relief means extra tax to help people." Is he right? | No, it means a tax cut | Yes, it is a new tax | Yes, it is a donation | No, it means no taxes at all ever
test: An old newspaper: "The plan was built for plausible deniability." What does it suggest? | Leaders could claim they did not know | The plan was very believable to everyone | Nobody could deny the plan | The plan was on the news
test: A politician promises "investment in our roads". What does it mean in plain words? | Government spending on roads | Buying shares in a road company | Selling the roads | Putting money in a bank
test: A news report says a city was emptied by "population transfer". What happened to the people? | They were forced to leave their homes | They moved for new jobs by choice | They were all counted for the census | They went on holiday
exam: Identify the principal function of the expression "mistakes were made" in an official statement. | To acknowledge error whilst concealing the responsible party | To deny that any error occurred | To attribute the error to a named official | To specify the date of the error
exam: It is given that two governments have issued a communiqué describing "a full and frank exchange of views". Select the response consistent with customary diplomatic usage. | The parties disagreed substantially | The parties concluded a binding treaty | The parties exchanged ceremonial gifts | The parties declined to meet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises "entitlement reform" as the term is employed in American politics? | The reduction of pensions and welfare provision | The augmentation of pensions and welfare provision | The renaming of official titles | The abolition of all taxation
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of "internally displaced person" as employed in international reports. | A person compelled to leave home who remains within his own country | A person who has emigrated voluntarily to a foreign land | A person confined indoors by illness | A person employed within a government building
